e  other flies Blake on the other hand is  watching everything and and it's so  you're telling two different sets of  stories there's two different ways of  coping with it as well and even within  that the relationship between the camera  sometimes it's the camera moving around  them sometimes it's them moving in  relation to the camera for example when  you go down into the big crater they  have to go around the perimeter of the  crater the camera skims across the  surface of the water so in that moment  you see in them small they disappear a  little bit into the mist then they  return again that sort of dance and you  know you've got a we weren't responding  to land because the land didn't exist we  had to create the land and then make the  shot within it and that's quite  different you know wasn't like we just  turned up and thought would saw no man  so I thought how we gonna shoot this we  have to make it out of nothing and then  find a way for the camera to Rachel  so sometimes it's the camera dictating  what the set should be and sometimes  it's the other way around but you can't  ever quite put your finger on what came  first and that's why it was such a  unified there's a team effort you know  is a group of us all working at it at  the same time the actors for the whole  of Pratt just rehearsing rehearsing just  walking those roots until we found what  we thought the answer was you co-wrote  the script and I'm curious how did the  script and story change once you started  being in prep and doing all the stuff  with camera moves and  the locations did things change a lot  the story never changed the story  structure was always the same I mean I  always felt very solid about what  happened when and to a certain degree  you can't really change it the trenches  are the trenches no-man's land so where  you know everything had to have a logic  so that didn't change but lots of little  things changed all the time and  particularly because I was watching the  two boys doing it I was able to let them  do it for weeks and just observe them  without directing them particularly hard  and yeah there were lots of little  things that adjustments we made to their  parts but which took it closer to them I  was watching what they were doing  naturally with each other and that way I  was able to make adjustments all the  time the things they probably weren't  even that aware of you know you don't  need to say that line just do that with  a look or you know maybe switch those  lines around and and it just happened  organically so by the time we came to  shoot they were row a rock-solid about  what they were doing what they were  saying how they were moving they've been  wearing their uniforms for six months  they've been handling their weapons  they've been to boot camp have been to  France they've been everywhere and they  were sort of in it and that wasn't  inappropriate because I was encouraging  them to sort of live it as much as to  act it I'm curious which scene which  which one was the toughest one to pick a  take on meaning like did you have a  certain scene that take 10 12 and 14  were all just amazing Oh nearly every  day there was literally you know I was  spoiled for choice and I think when Sam  and I were talking about it we might be  kicking around six brilliant takes you  know and then trying to whittle that  down to the one you know what one gets  to be the one yeah I mean I don't think  there was a day when it was just one  take like that we're always like nobody  talked about the last shot on the movie  because that was a really were very  particular about that though it's the  one that which is the first take that we  use and why oh yes  the one where Blake's brother schoo meet  Blake's brother at the end it's very  it's a highly emotional thing and I  think we had 9 or 12 takes and I  remember and I was watching it the first  take was incredibly emotional like I'm  sitting in Soho not on location looking  at a you know