r there are so many amazing shots in  this film I'm curious which one for you  do you I guess is there one that you  felt that was like just really  incredibly difficult to get right or one  you know what I mean if you could just  talk about some of the incredible work  you did well it's difficult for  different reasons I said the hardest  thing it's like I operate or is like you  know walking backwards with two guys in  a trench I mean that was a really hard  but but a lot of it that seems looks you  think was really hard was really hard  but I mean they're going across that  bridging Glasgow we worked it out I mean  we did a mock-up on the back lot in  Shepparton with scaffold towers and the  cranes to this a four-point wire rig and  at one point the cameras taken off the  one then they run with it and go up the  steps to the lock house I mean it looks  an incredibly hard shot and probably if  I watch it again in the year or two I  think my god that's really hard but  because we had so much prep we worked  out the shot Sam said we storyboarded it  a number of times worked out what we  wanted tested it on the back lot we went  to Glasgow and the shot was there the  guys had bricked it  the camera was it's controlled by you  know his computer program so they took  the same program from what we've done in  the back lot and Sheppard and and did it  in Glasgow so when we got there it was  all there I mean we changed the curve  and all that a little bit but it was  basically done so the time we got to  shooting these things on set we'd  rehearse them so many time I mean one of  the hardest shots was the singing  soldier I mean you start coming up the  hill and you don't end until you're  running towards the front line and that  whole choreographed shot of that moving  at sitting  incredibly hard shot for an operator to  do but we spent days and days in a field  with extras rehearsing that shot so by  the time we came to do it for the actors  it was it was pretty well programmed  when you're when you're doing that that  take that scene is there is so beautiful  when it's going on and it's so perfect  how scared are you that one of those  extras is just going to look at the lens  or just you know what I mean yeah it did  happen and you know some but the funny  thing is it's like I was saying earlier  you want to encourage a kind of freedom  as well so you don't want it to be too  sort of a lot you know people terrify  they're gonna [ __ ] up a take you know  and they just that they try too hard  they need to live in the space so you're  trying to actually weirdly encourage not  too much thought I mean a good example  of that is in one of the shots I would  say it's probably one of the most  difficult while certainly on the day was  was but it was exhilarating is that  final run that George takes down that  Scofield takes down the front line as  they're all pouring over the top and and  - you know when he gets knocked off his  feet that was not planned you know that  was extremely adrenaline extra thinking  thing it's what a camera you know and  but you know I'd had said to him  beforehand listen if somebody knocks you  over just keep trying to keep going you  know and he did and and it's in the  movie so that that's what you're looking  for you know and and that happened a few  times even though the camera was pretty  as Roger said we'd roast it so well you  you want life to enter in somehow you  won't mess and and you know there are  animals and babies and the weather  changes and the light changes and  conditions change and you fall in the  mud if you fall in the mud it's your  character falling in the mud get up  again you know and don't let it stop you  and that's obviously something badly  goes wrong you have to stop but most of  the I just encouraged him to get through  to the end and and knowing that knowing  that there was a likelihood I was I  wasn't going to say car and a half the  time if I'd said cut they wouldn't have  